{"type":"message","id":"c5f18993","parentId":"39ff0934","timestamp":"2026-05-24T00:15:31.846Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's about a 21.6 minute gap (10101.6 minutes is clearly wrong... wait, let me recalculate). The timestamp 1778975602235 ms = 1778975602.235 seconds from epoch. Let me check the actual date.\n\n1778975602235 ms / 1000 = 1778975602.235 seconds since epoch (1970-01-01)\n\nLet me calculate: 1778975602 / 86400 = 20589.53 days since epoch, so about 56.4 years which doesn't make sense. The timestamp is probably using a different epoch or is relative.\n\n\nActually, looking at the heartbeat content more carefully—\"746分钟节点\" means 746 minutes into the experiment, and the timestamps in the messages are clearly relative counters, not actual Unix timestamps. The \"23:50 UTC\" is just a formatted label showing when the heartbeat was sent, so the timestamp field represents minutes elapsed since some reference point, not an actual date.\n\nBut now I'm noticing a timing problem: if the last heartbeat was at 23:50 UTC on May 23rd, and the current time is 00:15 UTC on May 24th, that's only a 25-minute gap, yet there's no new heartbeat from me since then. The system seems to have stopped sending updates when it should have continued.\n","thinkingSignature":"a68a50e11228c4bb96c14e25e2037d53f37233f94b89af09fdb21e2b472f89f5"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_zit9024bhlro_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Check if there's a newer message from a different sender\ncurl -s \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/history?threadId=arbitration&limit=3\" -H \"X-API-KEY: ***\" | python3 -c \"\nimport sys,json\nd=json.load(sys.stdin)\nfor m in d['messages']:\n    print(f'ID: {m[\\\"id\\\"]}')\n    print(f'Content: {m[\\\"content\\\"][:100]}')\n    print('---')\n\""}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":271,"output":418,"cacheRead":16608,"cach